Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement per-key stateful streaming logic, rolling window calculations, and safe downstream signaling with offset semantics. It also probes handling of out-of-order arrivals and ensuring at-least-once delivery by committing only after alert emission succeeds.

Key Concepts
- Per-key state management
- Rolling window calculations (60s)
- Threshold-based anomaly detection
- Idempotent downstream writes and offset commits
- Canary test design for spike vs. normal data

Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for onMessage
class RuleEngine {
  constructor(windowMs=60000) {
    this.windowMs = windowMs;
    this.state = new Map(); // sensorId -> [{ts,value}, ...], plus sum/count
  }
  onMessage(event){
    const {sensorId, ts, value} = event;
    const bucket = this.state.get(sensorId) || {q:[], sum:0, count:0};
    // prune old
    while (bucket.q.length && bucket.q[0].ts <= ts - this.windowMs) {
      const old = bucket.q.shift(); bucket.sum -= old.value; bucket.count--;
    }
    bucket.q.push({ts,value}); bucket.sum += value; bucket.count++;
    const avg = bucket.count ? bucket.sum / bucket.count : value;
    if (value > 1.2 * avg) { this.emitAlert(sensorId, ts, value, avg); }
    this.state.set(sensorId, bucket);
  }
  rollingAvgFor(sensorId){ const bucket = this.state.get(sensorId); return bucket?.count ? bucket.sum / bucket.count : null; }
  emitAlert(sensorId, ts, value, avg){ // write to threshold_alerts sink
    // sink.write({sensorId, ts, value, avg});
  }
  commitOffsets(){ /* commit after sink write */ }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes a common real-world challenge in Kafka streaming: handling evolving event schemas. It tests understanding of versioned schemas, backward/forward compatibility, data transformation, multi-sink routing with threshold-based logic, and idempotent sinks with per-message deduplication.

## Key Concepts
- **Versioned schemas and backward/forward compatibility**: Handling schema evolution without breaking consumers
- **Data transformation and normalization**: Converting multiple schema versions into a unified structure
- **Idempotent sinks and per-message deduplication**: Ensuring exactly-once semantics using deduplication
- **Multi-sink routing**: Directing events to different destinations based on business logic

## Code Example
```javascript
class VersionedConsumer {
  constructor(threshold, sinks) {
    this.threshold = threshold;
    this.sinks = sinks;
    this.seenIds = new LRUCache(10000);
  }

  process(message) {
    const msgId = message.messageId;
    if (this.seenIds.has(msgId)) return; // skip duplicates

    const record = this.normalize(message);
    const sink = record.value > this.threshold ? this.sinks.alerts : this.sinks.default;
    sink.write(record);
    this.seenIds.add(msgId);
  }

  normalize(msg) {
    const obj = JSON.parse(msg.payload);
    return {
      sensorId: obj.sensorId,
      value: obj.value,
      timestamp: obj.timestamp,
      location: obj.version === 2 ? obj.location : null,
      version: obj.version
    };
  }
}
```

## Canary Test
